<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napata was an ancient city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> near the Fourth Cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. It became a principal centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> and before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, and housed an important cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> that legitimised royal power. Kushite kings who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as its Twenty-fifth Dynasty drew their authority from this religious centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napata.jpg" alt="Napata" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LassiHU · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amun_in_Barkal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Napata was a city of ancient Kush at the fourth cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, about 1.5 kilometres from the east bank, at the site of modern Karima in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Its Egyptian name, npt, was given it by the Egyptian cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> that founded it for Egyptian pilgrims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> founded it in the 15th century BCE after conquering Kush. It was the southernmost permanent settlement of New Kingdom Egypt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, a lone sandstone butte beside it, became the main Kushite cult centre of Amun, residence of the Egyptian state god Amun of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1075 BCE the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at Thebes weakened Egyptian control, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a>, increasingly estranged from the Theban clergy, regained autonomy and founded a kingdom centred at Napata. By about 750 BCE Napata was a developed city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashta">Kashta</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a> brought the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> under Kushite control. The Kushite kings ruled the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for about 57 years as the Twenty-fifth Dynasty, believing that their power came through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>. Taharqa and others built or restored temples at Memphis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, Kawa and Jebel Barkal. Conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> ended their rule, and the kings withdrew to Napata, where they were buried under pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-kurru">El-Kurru</a> and Nuri.</p><p>Napata remained the centre of Kush for two more generations, with an economy based on gold, and the Temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and Mut at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> were its most important. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> sacked it. The capital then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, an island area rich in iron, though Napata remained the primary religious centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 23 BCE the Roman governor of Egypt Gaius Petronius invaded Kush with 10,000 men and reached Napata, and Augustus claims in the Res Gestae that a penetration was made as far as the town. King Natakamani rebuilt it, renovating the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and building a palace, but the site was later abandoned and plundered. The American archaeologist George A. Reisner found ten complete or nearly complete statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> and his successors in two caches, one in the Amun temple, together with ash that suggested to him that they had been deliberately destroyed. The earliest known standing structure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> is the Enthronement Pavilion, dated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Napata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nubia under New Kingdom administration was changed so thoroughly that the later Kushite kingdom centred on <b>Napata</b> adopted Egyptian gods, titles, and monumental forms, and would eventually conquer Egypt itself as the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A new Kushite state took shape at <b>Napata</b>, near the holy mountain of Jebel Barkal, after Egyptian control of Nubia collapsed with the end of the New Kingdom around 1070 BCE and local elites reasserted independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…led a failed expedition into Arabia in 26–25 BCE, and when the Kushites raided the south and carried off statues of Augustus, the prefect Gaius Petronius marched up the Nile and sacked <b>Napata</b> in about 24 BCE, before a settlement at Samos in 21–20 BCE fixed the frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Nubia was governed by a Viceroy of Kush, the “King’s Son of Kush”, Egyptian towns were founded on Sai Island and at Sedeinga, Soleb, Mirgissa and Sesibi, and major ceremonies were held at Jebel Barkal near <b>Napata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeologists divide its ancient history into the A-Group (3700–2800 BCE), C-Group (2300–1600 BCE) and Kerma (2500–1500 BCE) cultures, followed by the age of the New Kingdom, <b>Napata</b> and Meroë.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
